<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyrus Cylinder</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cyrus Cylinder is an inscribed clay cylinder issued after the Persian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE. Written in Akkadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, it presents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> as chosen by the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and describes the restoration of temples and deported peoples. It is often cited as an early statement of tolerant rule, marking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>'s absorption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder.jpg" alt="Cyrus Cylinder" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prioryman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
